LXMF-rs (Reticulum transport and LXMF wire format)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Coyote depends on the `reticulum-rs-transport` and `lxmf-wire` crates from
LXMF-rs (https://github.com/FreeTAKTeam/LXMF-rs). Upstream declares
`license = "EPL-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later"` in its package metadata.

Coyote relies on the GPL-2.0-or-later arm of that disjunction, which is the
only arm compatible with Coyote's AGPL-3.0-only license (GPL-2.0-or-later
permits GPL-3.0, which AGPL-3.0 section 13 allows to be combined with AGPL
work). The EPL-2.0 arm is not compatible with the GNU General Public License
family and is not relied upon here.

Unresolved obligation. The LICENSE file shipped by LXMF-rs is the stock,
unmodified EPL-2.0 text. Its "Exhibit A - Form of Secondary Licenses Notice"
is left as the blank template, still carrying the literal placeholder
"{name license(s), version(s), and exceptions or additional permissions
here}" in place of a named Secondary License. The EPL-2.0 text itself states,
immediately below that exhibit, that "Simply including a copy of this
Agreement, including this Exhibit A is not sufficient to license the Source
Code under Secondary Licenses." The LXMF-rs repository ships no GPL license
text and makes no dual-licensing statement outside the package metadata, so
the GPL arm is at present asserted only in metadata, which is not itself a
grant of rights. Clarification has been requested from the upstream
maintainers. Until that is resolved, these dependencies are present for
development purposes and Coyote should not be distributed as a combined work
containing them.

Attribution note. The Reticulum layer of LXMF-rs appears to derive from
Beechat's MIT-licensed Reticulum-rs: `crates/libs/rns-core/src/hash.rs` is
byte-identical to the Beechat original, while the LXMF-rs tree contains no
mention of Beechat and does not reproduce the copyright and permission notice
that the MIT License requires to be retained. This is recorded as an observed
discrepancy, not as an established finding. If the derivation is confirmed,
the MIT notice must be restored upstream or reproduced here.

Transitive dependency. `reticulum-rs-transport` enables its default `storage`
feature, which pulls in `rusqlite` with upstream's `bundled` setting and
therefore compiles the SQLite C amalgamation into Coyote. `rusqlite` and
`libsqlite3-sys` are distributed under the MIT License, Copyright (c) The
rusqlite developers. SQLite itself is in the public domain.

